ARM64EC: Remove writable-code tracking when executable pages become read-only.

The protection tracker added executable pages to RWXIntervals when they
became writable, but retained that interval when they changed back to RX.
GuestProtection then mistook the program's real RX protection for FEX's
write trap and reported RWX. After WriteProcessMemory temporarily made an
RX page writable and restored it, subsequent writes skipped that temporary
protection change and failed with ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED.

Remove the writable interval when the new executable protection is not
writable. Keep the executable interval and normal code invalidation.

Regression: tests/x64-fidelity/memtest.c, code.writeprocessmemory.
M4 r6 control: 49 pass / 1 fail (249 of 250 writes failed after the first).
Patched: 50 / 0. Other fidelity results are unchanged, including the seven
existing x87 failures in each architecture. FH6 gameplay remains unverified.

diff --git a/Source/Windows/Common/InvalidationTracker.cpp b/Source/Windows/Common/InvalidationTracker.cpp
index 342525a..705fb92 100644
--- a/Source/Windows/Common/InvalidationTracker.cpp
+++ b/Source/Windows/Common/InvalidationTracker.cpp
@@ -92,6 +92,9 @@ void InvalidationTracker::HandleMemoryProtectionNotification(uint64_t Address, u
       if (EffectiveRWX) {
         LogMan::Msg::DFmt("Add SMC interval: {:X} - {:X}", AlignedBase, AlignedBase + AlignedSize);
         RWXIntervals.Insert(ProtInterval);
+      } else {
+        // A transition from RWX to RX must stop treating FEX's RX trap as guest RWX.
+        RWXIntervals.Remove(ProtInterval);
       }
       if (DEPDisabled && !HasExec) {
         DEPPromotedIntervals.Insert(ProtInterval);
